diff --git a/docs/bots.md b/docs/bots.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e3c3ec7
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/bots.md
@@ -0,0 +1,269 @@
+# Chat bots
+
+Any member can open **Account settings → Set up chat bots** and create a bot.
+Members manage their own bots; administrators can manage all bots. The list shows
+every bot on one page. A bot's name and optional avatar can be changed without
+changing its code.
+
+Bots start with no room memberships. Select a bot in a room's member picker to
+add it to that open or closed room, or start a direct conversation with it.
+People still join open rooms automatically. A bot must belong to a room to post.
+
+The new/edit bot form's **Add to all open rooms** checkbox is off by default.
+Enabling it adds the active bot to every existing open room and to future open
+rooms (including closed rooms made open). Disabling it does not remove any
+memberships; it only stops automatic additions. Bots with this preference enabled
+cannot be deselected in an open room's picker. Existing bots keep their current
+memberships when this feature is installed and start with the preference off.
+
+## Posting
+
+Send a POST request to `/rooms/<room-id>/<bot-code>/messages`:
+
+```sh
+curl --data-binary 'Deploy of v1.2.3 finished ✅' \
+  "https://chat.example.com/rooms/${ROOM_ID}/${BOT_CODE}/messages"
+```
+
+The request body is UTF-8 plain text, not JSON or form fields. Any Content-Type
+is accepted as raw text, including curl's default form encoding. HTML is treated
+as literal text and line breaks are retained. The endpoint requires no browser
+session or CSRF token. Other browser forms retain their normal authentication
+and CSRF protection.
+
+Responses have empty bodies:
+
+| Status | Meaning |
+| --- | --- |
+| `201 Created` | Message created; `Location: /messages/:message_id` |
+| `422 Unprocessable Content` | Empty/whitespace-only body (or invalid UTF-8) |
+| `404 Not Found` | Room missing, malformed room ID, or bot is not a member |
+| `401 Unauthorized` | Invalid code, deleted bot, or inactive bot |
+
+Authentication is checked before room access. Messages use the standard room
+receive callback and broadcast flow, including unread state and push delivery.
+The author is the bot, not its creator.
+
+## Credentials
+
+Treat the bot code as a password. Keep it in a CI secret store or a protected
+environment variable; do not commit it to source control. Use HTTPS. Deleting a
+bot revokes its code; create a replacement if a code has been compromised.
+Deletion also removes the bot's memberships and messages, following the existing
+user destruction associations.
+
+Bot management responses disable HTTP and Turbo page caching. Rails parameter,
+SQL, and request-path logs redact the code, as do Sentry request URLs. Reverse
+proxies, access logs, CI command tracing, and external monitoring must also be
+configured not to record this credential-bearing URL.
+
+## Implementation notes
+
+`Bot < User` uses single-table inheritance, so avatars, room membership, mentions,
+message authorship, and notifications share the existing user infrastructure.
+Bots track their creator and have a unique, random 32-character code. Human
+account administration and all human sign-in paths exclude bots.
+
+`BotRequests` normalizes Content-Type before Rails parameter parsing. This is
+important for curl's form encoding and prevents a JSON parser from rejecting raw
+text. It also redacts credential-bearing paths, which ordinary Rails parameter
+filtering does not cover.
+
+Regression coverage is in the bot model/controller tests, `BotRequestsTest`, and
+`test/system/bots_test.rb` (native confirmation and live author/broadcast checks).
+
+## Mention webhooks
+
+On the new or edit bot form, set **Webhook URL** to an HTTP(S) endpoint. This is
+optional; clearing the field removes the webhook without changing the bot code.
+Bots appear alongside people in the room's @-mention autocomplete. Select the
+bot there: a literal `@Bot name` typed as ordinary text is not a structured mention.
+
+Each new, committed human message mentioning an active bot in that room queues
+one callback to its webhook, even if it mentions that bot several times. HTTP
+and DNS run in a background job, never in the message-posting request. Production
+requires the normal Resque workers. Bot-authored messages do not trigger
+callbacks, preventing reply loops. Editing a message does not send a new event.
+
+The callback is an HTTP POST with `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "mention",
+  "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages"
+  },
+  "message": {
+    "id": 118,
+    "body": {
+      "text": "restart the build please",
+      "html": "<div class=\"trix-content\">… full rich-text body …</div>"
+    },
+    "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+`text` removes only that bot's structured mentions and trims surrounding
+whitespace; other mentions, literal text, and line breaks remain. `html` is the
+full rendered rich-text body, including the bot mention. Each mentioned bot gets
+its own command text and posting URL. All endpoint/link paths are relative to the
+Campfire origin, which the bot must know.
+
+Return `200` with `Content-Type: text/plain` or `text/html` (charset parameters are
+accepted) to post the response as the bot in the same room. Plain text is escaped
+and line breaks preserved; HTML uses normal Action Text rendering/sanitization.
+Replies are broadcast and update unread state/push notifications just like other
+messages. Return `204` for no immediate reply. To reply later, send a raw UTF-8
+plain-text POST to `room.reply_to` as described above. That URL contains the bot's
+secret code: protect the callback and prefer HTTPS.
+
+Other statuses/content types, empty or invalid UTF-8 bodies, and replies over
+1 MiB do not create messages. Delivery uses bounded connection/read/write
+inactivity timeouts and does not automatically retry or follow redirects. Like
+link unfurling, webhooks use the app's public-address policy to prevent requests
+to private/internal networks, with the resolved address pinned to the connection.
+The URL cannot contain userinfo or a fragment. Clearing the webhook, deactivating
+the bot, removing its room membership or mention, or deleting it/the message
+before the job runs cancels delivery. Network failures count toward suspension and produce a sanitized
+warning containing bot/message IDs and the exception class, not the URL or payload.
+
+Implementation: `Message::Mentionee` queues `Bot::WebhookJob`, `Bot::Webhook`
+builds/delivers the payload, and `Bot#post_message_in` shares the posting/broadcast
+path between immediate and out-of-band replies. Regression tests also cover
+queued cancellation, payload paths, safe reply handling, and browser autocomplete.
+
+## Joined webhooks
+
+Each bot's first addition to an open or closed room, whether selected by a person
+or added automatically, queues a webhook POST with this payload:
+
+```json
+{
+  "event": "joined",
+  "room": {
+    "id": 5,
+    "name": "Ops",
+    "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages"
+  }
+}
+```
+
+Direct conversations never produce `joined`. There is no author or message in
+this event. The same `200` plain-text/HTML response handling and `204`/later-reply
+behavior as mention webhooks applies, including broadcasts, unread state, push
+notifications, and all network/reply safety limits.
+
+Delivery is queued only after the membership transaction commits, and only for
+active bots with a webhook configured at that time. Removing the webhook,
+deactivating/deleting the bot, revoking room access, or deleting the room before
+execution cancels delivery. Adding a webhook later does not replay historical
+joins.
+
+### Implementation notes
+
+`Room#memberships.grant_to` keeps bulk inserts for people but uses idempotent,
+callback-backed creates for bots. `Membership` records a `Bot::RoomJoin` inside
+the membership transaction; its post-commit callback queues
+`Bot::JoinedWebhookJob`. Do not bulk-insert bot memberships, since that would
+bypass joined notifications.
+
+A unique bot/room pair in `bot_room_joins` outlives the membership, so repeated
+room edits, preference toggles, or removing and re-adding the bot do not send a
+second greeting to that room. Bot/room deletion cleans up these records. The
+job atomically claims one delivery attempt before HTTP to avoid duplicate
+requests/replies if the job is executed twice. As with mentions, failed deliveries
+are not retried; exactly-once *successful* receipt cannot be guaranteed over HTTP.
+Both event types share `Bot::Webhook` and `Bot#post_message_in`.
+
+Regression coverage for membership preferences, transactional/idempotent joins,
+cancellations, and webhook replies:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/bot test/jobs/bot test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb test/controllers/rooms
+```
+
+`bin/rails test test/system/bots_test.rb` covers the visible checkbox and a live
+joined greeting alongside the existing bot management/mention browser flows.
+
+## Per-member bot limits
+
+Administrators set **Bots per member** on `/account/edit`. The integer setting
+is stored as `Account#settings.bots_per_member` and defaults to 5, including for
+accounts created before the setting existed. Zero disables new bots for members.
+Administrators' own bots are unlimited. Every undeleted bot counts against its
+creator's quota; deleting a bot frees a slot. Lowering the setting never deletes
+bots or prevents editing existing ones.
+
+`User#can_create_bot?` controls the add link and access to the new-bot page.
+`Bot` also validates the creator's quota **on create**, so direct POSTs and forms
+opened before a quota change cannot bypass it. Keep this check in the model, not
+only in the controller. Normal saves run the count and insertion in the same
+transaction; the configured SQLite immediate transaction mode serializes writers.
+If the database adapter or transaction mode changes, preserve that serialization
+when enforcing quotas.
+
+Focused regression coverage:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/account/bot_limits_test.rb test/models/bot/creation_limit_test.rb test/controllers/accounts_controller_test.rb test/controllers/bots/creation_limits_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bot_limits_test.rb
+```
+
+## Automatic bot safeguards
+
+These limits are fixed in `Bot::Safeguards`; there are no account settings for them.
+
+* A bot may post **10 messages in any 10 seconds**, across all its rooms. API
+  posts and both mention/joined webhook replies share the same sliding window.
+* The next attempt is rejected and starts a **30-second cooldown**. The API
+  returns `429 Too Many Requests` with `Retry-After` rounded up to whole seconds.
+  Retries do not extend the deadline. Webhooks can still be delivered, but their
+  replies cannot post during the cooldown.
+* **Five additional posting attempts in that cooldown** suspend the bot. Both
+  API attempts and nonempty, valid webhook replies count; the triggering attempt
+  that starts the cooldown does not count toward these five. The fifth additional
+  API attempt returns `403 Forbidden` immediately.
+* **Ten consecutive failed webhook deliveries** also suspend the bot. Any
+  non-`2xx` response (including redirects), timeout, or connection error counts.
+  Any `2xx` delivery resets the failure count, even when it has no usable reply
+  or its reply is throttled. Joined and mention deliveries share this counter.
+
+Suspension is a separate user status, not deactivation or a ban. It preserves
+memberships, messages, bot codes, join-delivery history, and the owner's bot quota
+usage. Suspended bots remain on the bots page with **Suspended** and the reason.
+The posting endpoint returns `403`, and queued/new webhooks are suppressed.
+HTTP requests already in flight cannot be unsent, but their replies are checked
+again before posting and cannot clear a suspension.
+
+Only administrators see **Reactivate** on the bots page. It uses Turbo's native
+browser confirmation and an administrator-only POST route. Reactivation clears
+the suspension reason, cooldown, cooldown attempts, and webhook failure counter;
+it does not replay missed webhooks or joins. Recent successful posting timestamps
+remain subject to the normal ten-second window, so reactivation cannot bypass
+that limit. Owners can edit a suspended bot but cannot reactivate it through edits.
+
+### Persistence and concurrency
+
+The bot row stores the cooldown deadline, counters, reason, and a bounded JSON
+list of at most ten recent posting timestamps. This history survives deletion of
+messages and is pruned when posting succeeds. It does not rely on process-local
+state, cache TTLs, or the job worker used for delivery.
+
+`Bot#post_message_in` holds `with_lock` across the rate check, message insertion,
+and timestamp update. Rejection errors are raised **after** the transaction, so
+cooldown/suspension changes are not rolled back. Webhook outcomes and reactivation
+also lock/reload the same row. SQLite's configured immediate transaction mode
+serializes writers; keep this behavior (or row locks on other adapters) when
+changing the database. HTTP delivery happens outside the lock, and message
+broadcasts happen after creation commits.
+
+Regression coverage:
+
+```sh
+bin/rails test test/models/bot test/jobs/bot test/controllers/bots test/controllers/bots_controller_test.rb
+bin/rails test test/system/bot_safeguards_test.rb
+```
diff --git a/lib/bot_requests.rb b/lib/bot_requests.rb
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..3a2ea89
--- /dev/null
+++ b/lib/bot_requests.rb
@@ -0,0 +1,28 @@
+# Bot messages are raw text, regardless of the sender's Content-Type. Normalize
+# before Rails logging/parameter parsing, including curl's default form encoding.
+class BotRequests
+  MESSAGE_PATH = %r{\A(/rooms/[^/]+/)[^/]+(/messages)(?=\?|\z)}
+
+  def self.redact_url(url)
+    url&.sub(%r{(/rooms/[^/?#]+/)[^/?#]+(/messages)(?=[?#]|\z)}, '\1[FILTERED]\2')
+  end
+
+  def initialize(app)
+    @app = app
+  end
+
+  def call(env)
+    if env["REQUEST_METHOD"] == "POST" && MESSAGE_PATH.match?(env["PATH_INFO"])
+      env["CONTENT_TYPE"] = "text/plain"
+    end
+
+    @app.call(env)
+  end
+
+  # Parameter filtering alone doesn't redact credentials in path segments.
+  module FilteredPath
+    def filtered_path
+      BotRequests.redact_url(super)
+    end
+  end
+end
